  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA slow moving low pressure system propels a front towards the area \ntoday, then the closed low fades into a trough which continues to \nshift east overhead early in the work week. Another closed low is \nprojected to develop to the south of the forecast area, but this too \nweakens as it travels north and is absorbed into a longwave trough \nmid week. For the rest of the week into next weekend, high pressure \nwill be present over the southwest US and off the Atlantic coast \nwhile the longwave trough skims the north-central and northeastern \nUS. Shortwaves traveling along the southern edge of this trough will \nkeep unsettled conditions present through much of the week.\n\nFor today, scattered showers and thunderstorms are expected to \ncontinue through the morning. More robust activity then develops as \ninstability become moderate to strong during the afternoon and \nevening hours. This increase in instability, combined with decent \nshear and lapse rates, should facilitate isolated to scattered \nsevere thunderstorms. While the primary hazard will be damaging wind \ngusts, there will also be a low-end risk for tornados. \n\nAbundant moisture will also make showers and thunderstorms rather \nefficient rainfall producers that could prompt flash flooding across \nportions of the area today. Most susceptible will be poor-drainage \nareas and locations impacted by training storms. Given this risk, a \nFlood Watch has been issued for much of West Virginia from 10 AM \ntoday through tonight. \n\nActivity is expected to taper off as instability wanes overnight. \nHowever, the unsettled pattern mentioned above is projected to \nfacilitate additional chances of diurnal showers and general \nthunderstorms most days this week.",
